# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# PyInstaller build output
build/
dist/

# The built executable. Tracked only on `main`, and only at a release
# (see docs/RELEASING.md) - that is the copy the in-app updater serves,
# and the only one anybody downloads. On `development` it is an artifact
# like any other: rebuildable from source at any time with
# `python packaging/build.py`, and 47MB of permanent repository weight
# every time it is committed. Adding it to a release commit needs
# `git add -f`, since this rule would otherwise skip it.
/Atomic.exe
/Atomic.zip

# The video player's decode engine (libmpv-2.dll, ~120MB unpacked).
# Same argument as Atomic.exe above: it is a rebuildable artifact, not
# source, and committing it would put 120MB into every clone forever.
# Fetch it with `python packaging/fetch_libmpv.py`; the build refuses to
# run without it rather than shipping a player that cannot decode.
/vendor/

# User data: personal tracker lists, settings (incl. Stremio auth key/
# email once connected), cached cover images, site config - none of this
# belongs in source control, and dev data is auto-recreated on first run.
data/

# Local Claude Code session settings (machine-specific permissions)
.claude/settings.local.json

# Editor/OS cruft
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# The owner's TMDB read token is *committed* now, at
# packaging/tmdb_token.txt, and is deliberately not ignored here.
#
# It used to be ignored, with the encrypted tmdb_token.txt.enc beside it
# as the way to move it between machines. That broke down the first time
# it was actually needed: a second machine could not build, the
# passphrase was not to hand, and no released exe carried the token to
# recover it from either - v1.10 was tagged two days before the bundling
# existed. The owner's call, 21 August 2026, was to commit it plainly.
#
# The risk that decision accepts is not that someone uses the key - it
# is that a scanner finds it and TMDB revokes it, which blanks artwork
# for every copy at once. If that happens, the fix is a new key from
# themoviedb.org/settings/api, not a hunt for a bug. And it is in the
# history from now on, so removing the file later does not unpublish it;
# rotating the key is what does.

# The owner's Real-Debrid token is the opposite call, and deliberately
# so: the TMDB token is a free read-only metadata key, this is a *paid
# account credential*, and on a public repository "committed" means
# "published". It reaches users inside the built exe (Atomic.spec
# bundles it when the file exists) and never through the repository.
# The owner places the file himself; a tree without it builds fine and
# simply ships an exe whose debrid stays dark until a key is pasted in
# Settings.
packaging/rd_token.txt

# Window-event logs from the diagnostic rig used to chase the flashing
# windows (packaging has no use for them; one got committed once).
/events.jsonl
*.jsonl

# WebView2's own user-data folder. Edge creates it beside the exe the
# first time a page is hosted (helpers/webview2_host) and fills it with
# a browser profile - a cache, cookies, and a few thousand files. It is
# per-machine state, never source.
/Atomic.exe.WebView2/
*.WebView2/

# Vendored beside libmpv for the same reason: fetched, not written here.
# Unlike libmpv these are small and the build needs them, so they stay
# tracked - this line is only a reminder of where they came from.
#   vendor/webview2/  <- tracked on purpose (packaging/Atomic.spec)

# Run logs from driving the app during development.
/debug.log
/run_src.log
/probe.log
/cover.log
